WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.410
Grab your Ashley Too dolls...

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:03.100
(These are not dolls, Jim)

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:06.820
...and contain your excitement, because we have an all-new season of Black Mirror.

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:12.600
In this video we'll be looking at season five's episode three: Rachel, Jack and Ashley Too

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.900
--which is the origin story for Hannah Montana

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:15.760
(not really, thank God)

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:18.880
So get ready, as we'll be taking a deep dive into the ending.

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:23.820
And if you want to skip ahead just to the Easter eggs, I posted a time stamp in the description below.

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:26.900
To better understand the ending we have to rewind to the beginning.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:33.200
Unlike traditional Black Mirror stories, which tend to follow one character, this episode follows three.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:38.220
It's so important the name of the episode is literally named after those three characters.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.380
Now, you might think that Rachel is the main character here.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:45.520
After all, we start on her, spend most of the beginning with her, and the narrative is largely pushed forward

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:50.720
by her obsession with the Ashley doll, but I'll go over in a bit why I think that's not the case.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.480
Rachel's starting at a new school and doesn't have a lot of friends.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:59.070
She's lonely not just because of her lack of friends, but she's lost her mother, too.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:04.500
She lacks confidence, and when someone asks her whether she's gonna sign up for the talent show, she says "no".

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:08.480
But Rachel's defining feature is her obsession with pop star Ashley O

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:15.260
a woman who seemingly has the perfect superstar life, and in Rachel's mind is something she wants to become.

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:19.340
She practices her dance moves, listens to all her songs, and asks her dad,

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:23.200
who doesn't even know it's her birthday coming up, to get her an Ashley Too doll.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:28.100
And part of the reason Rachel loves Ashley so much is her positive message and outlook on life.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:32.680
Her lyrics are upbeat and empowering, saying things like "you're on a roll"

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:34.500
and "you're gonna get what you deserve".

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.060
As Ashley says in her interview:

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:41.940
"it's really important for people to feel like they're in control of their own destiny...

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:46.280
"and, you know, that means having the confidence to be who you want to be".

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:50.120
The irony here is that Ashley herself isn't in control of her destiny;

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:53.860
she's trapped in this fake world pretending to be something she isn't.

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:57.620
She lives in a glass house, just like one of Kevin's mice,

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:01.900
and is controlled by her domineering manager/aunt, Catherine.

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:08.140
If that isn't clear enough, she composes her own music, writing "see the animal in the cage you've built".

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:13.120
While talking to the janitor at Rachel's high school, Kevin explains the inhumanity of poisoned mousetraps,

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:15.800
and how they kill these creatures slowly.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:20.520
Ashley's poison is her medication, from her aunt, which slowly eats away at her too.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:26.060
She yearns to be free, which is best personified when she stares longingly at the rock-and-roll venue.

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:31.940
Like Rachel, Ashley has no friends. They are oddly both similar and dissimilar at the same time...

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:33.960
But back to the mice analogy.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:38.440
It doesn't stop at Ashley; both Rachel and Jack feel trapped, too.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:41.460
Rachel is trapped in a new school, unable to make friends,

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:45.900
while Jack is trapped in the past, unable to get over the death of her mother,

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:49.260
and unable to muster the courage to play her guitar for others.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:54.040
As Rachel says, "sure, ever since she died all you do is mope around with that stupid guitar,

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:57.380
"which you're probably no good at 'cause you're too scared to play in front of anyone".

00:02:57.380 --> 00:03:01.200
It's here that I think Jack shifts is the real protagonist of the story.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:05.720
You see, Rachel wants to have friends, and to be able to muster the courage to perform at her talent show.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:10.360
She achieves both of these about halfway through the episode, although not in the way we'd expect.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:14.380
She fails miserably at the talent show and her only friend becomes a robot.

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:17.380
Not to mention that Rachel is fairly passive in this story;

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:21.080
things happen to her instead of her making things happen.

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:26.580
Her character, when compared with Jack and Ashley, changes the least by the end of the episode.

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:27.940
But let's look at Jack's arc.

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:31.800
Jack wants to get over the death of her mother and to find her own place in the world.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:36.260
She even reads Jim Dodge's "Stone Junction", which is a coming-of-age tale

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:39.000
in which the protagonist tries to find his place in the world.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:42.220
But the whole getting over the death of her mother is personified through her

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:45.080
gaining the courage to play her guitar at the end of the episode.

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:50.580
Not only that, but the irony of her playing alongside the woman she made fun of her sister for adoring

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:51.480
is not lost on us.

00:03:51.489 --> 00:03:56.849
But Jack is more of a protagonist simply because she propels the story forward with her actions.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.400
For example, she hides the Ashley doll, steals her dad's van, and

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:03.660
smashes Dr. Monk in the back of the head with a tray.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:08.240
Jack hides her grief in music, rock-and-roll, septum piercings, and dark clothes.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:13.120
A contrast to Rachel who prefers light upbeat pop with empowering messages.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:16.640
At the end of the episode, her journey has brought her on stage with Ashley

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:19.720
and she is finally playing, moving on with her life.

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:22.800
But it's Ashley's character who goes through the most visible arc.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:26.940
She's a woman who, quote, feels like she's wearing someone else's skin,

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:29.480
trapped in version of herself which isn't who she is.

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:34.190
In fact, the part of her that her aunt likes, the one that's great at doing press junkets and interviews,

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:37.000
constitutes only four percent of her brain.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:41.420
Which leads us to the main part of the story: the rise of Ashley Eternal.

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:46.100
When Catherine learns she can no longer control Ashley, she takes matters into her own hands

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:48.900
and she has several motivations for doing so.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:51.400
One, Ashley hasn't written an album in a while,

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:54.340
and the stuff she's been coming out with lately is drivel.

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:58.200
As she says to her colleagues, she's Ashley O, not Leonard Cohen.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:01.560
Two, they need a way to recoup their losses from the Ashley Too dolls.

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:03.820
And the perfect way for that is a new album.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:06.560
Three, Catherine feels like she's owed this.

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:11.260
She took Ashley in when her parents died and feels responsible for Ashley's success.

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:14.880
In her mind, she molded Ashley into the success she is today.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:19.120
So when Ashley tells her aunt that she's breaking her contract, she takes drastic measures

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:22.780
and poisons her own niece to put her in a medically induced coma.

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:25.720
This way, they can access the creative part of her brain and,

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:29.140
using some new software, continue to compose her music.

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:31.620
Remember that Ashley gets her song ideas from her dreams,

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:34.080
so if they can access that, they're in business.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:40.180
But there's just one problem: they can compose, but they no longer have Ashley's physical form.

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:41.920
Introducing: Ashley Eternal.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:46.460
A breakthrough hologram technology which would allow for simultaneous concerts

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:49.120
around the globe, and did I mention scalability?

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:53.340
It's only when the doll overhears a news broadcast involving Ashley's coma

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:55.240
does it go haywire and malfunction.

00:05:55.240 --> 00:06:00.040
The girls take it to their father's lab, hoping to fix it, but instead unlock its entire memory.

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:04.480
Apparently, every Ashley Too doll was instilled with the entirety of Ashley's personality,

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:07.080
but were given what's called a limiter.

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:11.540
The limiter would only allow the perky, fun Ashley image that fans know,

00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:13.780
while suppressing everything else.

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:16.360
But now that it's been removed, the Ashley Too doll tells them the truth:

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:20.780
that there was no way her coma was caused by an allergic reaction to shellfish.

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:23.900
So the girls do the only logical thing and steal their dad's truck

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:27.900
to break into Ashley's house and find proof that her aunt is responsible.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:32.440
Apparently, Ashley's laptop has a backup of her diary and will contain this proof,

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:35.000
But, as we'll find out later, this isn't the case.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:37.540
Ashley Too isn't here to get proof of anything,

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:43.039
She's here to put an end to her own suffering, and ends pulling the life-support plug in her own body.

00:06:43.039 --> 00:06:47.179
Now, this is interesting because it's almost as if you were to pull the plug on yourself;

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:49.940
it's Ashley's own consciousness in that doll.

00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:52.100
As she tells Rachel about her decision,

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:58.420
"Rachel, listen, I know me, okay? And I know I wouldn't wanna exist even ten minutes in that state!"

00:06:58.420 --> 00:07:01.240
But things aren't always what they seem in Black Mirror.

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:05.580
Ashley wakes up seemingly from the dead and scares the crap out of Rachel and Jack.

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:11.200
She wasn't on life support, she was being medically sedated in order for Catherine to exploit her.

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:15.060
They escape from Dr. Monk and speed off to confront Catherine at her investors meeting,

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:17.360
where she's unveiling Ashley Eternal.

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:20.040
Here, we can see some of the features, which include extended and

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:24.820
enhanced physical life, the ability to perform in harsh environments like the moon,

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:27.220
and the ability to control its consciousness.

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:31.420
Gone will be the days of performers getting sick, an AI infused with the artist

00:07:31.420 --> 00:07:35.680
can manifest itself in hologram form and perform anywhere around the globe and beyond.

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:39.880
All that's needed is a dude and a motion capture suit, and you're good to go.

00:07:40.099 --> 00:07:44.778
Oh, and costume changes are real easy and the girls come in like a wrecking ball

00:07:45.149 --> 00:07:48.739
Totally crashing the investor meeting here Catherine knows the gig is up

00:07:49.979 --> 00:07:56.389
We also get this little post credit sequence showing Ashley embracing her true self performing alongside Jack at the club

00:07:56.389 --> 00:08:02.239
She longingly stared at at the beginning of the episode. She's no longer trapped floating freely above the crowd

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:04.669
We also get Kevin trying to sell his shitty rat

00:08:04.889 --> 00:08:10.128
contraption and the two girls who were seen crying over Ashley's allergic reaction who aren't too thrilled about

00:08:10.199 --> 00:08:13.878
Ashley's new look and that's the point. This story is about being yourself

00:08:14.129 --> 00:08:17.389
Not what others want you to be now for a little bit of fun

